            "summary": "Cardinal extreme points of India: Indira Col (north), Cape Comorin (south mainland), Indira Point / Pygmalion Point (south overall), Kibithu (east), Guhar Moti / Sir Creek (west), and critical strategic border junctions.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>The Four Cardinal Extreme Points of India:</strong>\nThe geographical perimeter of the Indian Union is defined by four cardinal extremities located at its outermost frontiers:</p>\n<p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:8px; margin-bottom:12px;\" border=\"1\">\n<tr style=\"background:#f4f4f4;\">\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Cardinal Direction</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Geographic Place Name</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Administrative Jurisdiction</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Coordinates & Geographic Details</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Northernmost Point</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Indira Col</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Ladakh (near Siachen Glacier)</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">$37^\\circ 06'\\text{ N}$, $76^\\circ 47'\\text{ E}$; situated on the Indira Ridge in the Saltoro range of the Karakoram, near the trijunction of India, Pakistan, and China. (Named by Bullock Workman in 1912 after Goddess Lakshmi, not Indira Gandhi).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Southernmost Point (Mainland)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Cape Comorin (Kanyakumari)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Tamil Nadu</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">$8^\\circ 04'\\text{ N}$, $77^\\circ 35'\\text{ E}$; the confluence point where the Arabian Sea, Indian Ocean, and Bay of Bengal converge.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Southernmost Point (Overall)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Indira Point (Pygmalion Point / Parsons Point)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Great Nicobar Island (Andaman & Nicobar Islands)</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">$6^\\circ 45'\\text{ N}$, $93^\\circ 49'\\text{ E}$; located approximately $150\\text{ km}$ north of Sumatra (Indonesia). The light tower was partially submerged during the December 26, 2004 Indian Ocean Tsunami.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Easternmost Point</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Kibithu (Dong Valley)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Anjaw District, Arunachal Pradesh</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">$97^\\circ 25'\\text{ E}$, $28^\\circ 01'\\text{ N}$; situated on the Line of Actual Control (LAC) along the bank of the Lohit River. Dong receives the first rays of sunrise in India.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Westernmost Point</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Guhar Moti / Ghuar Mota (near Narayan Sarovar / Koteshwar)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">Kutch District, Gujarat</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\">$68^\\circ 07'\\text{ E}$, $23^\\circ 42'\\text{ N}$; situated on the marshy tidal wetlands near Sir Creek in the Rann of Kutch.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Critical International Trijunctions of India:</strong>\nStrategic geographic points where the international boundaries of three distinct sovereign nations meet:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1. India – Pakistan – China Trijunction:</strong> Located in northern Ladakh near the Karakoram Pass / Siachen region (Shaksgam Valley ceded illegally by Pakistan to China in 1963).</li>\n<li><strong>2. India – China – Myanmar Trijunction (Diphu Pass):</strong> Located at the eastern tip of Arunachal Pradesh on the McMahon Line near Diphu (Dipher) Pass.</li>\n<li><strong>3. India – Myanmar – Bangladesh Trijunction:</strong> Located at the southern tip of Mizoram in the Mizo / Lushai Hills.</li>\n<li><strong>4. India – Nepal – China Trijunctions:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li><em>Western Trijunction:</em> Located near <strong>Lipulekh Pass / Om Parvat</strong> (Pithoragarh district, Uttarakhand), subject to the Kalapani territorial dispute.</li>\n    <li><em>Eastern Trijunction:</em> Located near <strong>Jongsong Peak</strong> at the Sikkim-Nepal-Tibet boundary.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>5. India – Bhutan – China Trijunction (Doklam Plateau):</strong> Located near the Batang La / Doka La pass at the Sikkim-Bhutan-Tibet border. Scene of the 73-day military standoff between Indian and Chinese armed forces in 2017.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Geographical Elevation Extremes of India:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Highest Terrestrial Point:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>K2 (Mount Godwin-Austen / Qogir, $8,611\\text{ m}$):</strong> In the Karakoram Range (located in Pakistan-occupied Kashmir / Gilgit-Baltistan, claimed by India). 2nd highest peak in the world.</li>\n    <li><strong>Kanchenjunga ($8,586\\text{ m}$):</strong> Located on the border between Sikkim and Nepal. The highest mountain peak in undisputed Indian-administered territory (3rd highest in the world).</li>\n    <li><strong>Nanda Devi ($7,816\\text{ m}$):</strong> In Chamoli district, Uttarakhand. The highest mountain peak situated entirely within undisputed Indian borders.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lowest Terrestrial Point:</strong>\n  <strong>Kuttanad</strong> (Alappuzha and Kottayam districts, Kerala). Situated at approximately <strong>$2.2\\text{ meters below sea level}$ ($-2.2\\text{ m}$)</strong>. Renowned as the \"Rice Bowl of Kerala\", it is one of the few places in the world where farming is practiced below sea level (recognized by FAO as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System / GIAHS).\n</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Northernmost point: Indira Col (Ladakh, 37°6' N); Southernmost mainland: Cape Comorin (Tamil Nadu, 8°4' N).",
                "Southernmost point overall: Indira Point (Great Nicobar, 6°45' N), partly submerged in 2004 tsunami.",
                "Easternmost point: Kibithu in Anjaw district, Arunachal Pradesh (97°25' E); Westernmost point: Guhar Moti in Kutch, Gujarat (68°7' E).",
                "Highest peak in Indian territory: K2 (8,611 m in PoK); Highest peak in undisputed Indian territory: Kanchenjunga (8,586 m in Sikkim).",
                "Lowest point in India: Kuttanad in Kerala (2.2 meters below sea level)."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Civil Services Key Question: Indira Col was named in 1912 by workwoman Bullock Workman after Goddess Lakshmi (Indira), not after Prime Minister Indira Gandhi. Indira Point was officially renamed in honor of Indira Gandhi in 1985."
            ],
