            "id": "m04",
            "title": "The Tropic of Cancer & Its Geographic Trajectory Across India",
            "summary": "Latitude 23°30' N, division of India into tropical and subtropical zones, traversal across 8 states from west to east, Mahi River cutting it twice, proximity of state capitals (Ranchi on the line), and climatic implications.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>The Tropic of Cancer (23°30' N):</strong>\nThe <strong>Tropic of Cancer</strong> is the parallel of latitude situated at exactly <strong>$23^\\circ 30'\\text{ N}$</strong> ($23.5^\\circ\\text{ N}$) of the Equator. It marks the northernmost latitude on the Earth where the Sun can appear directly overhead (at zenith) at solar noon, which occurs annually on the <strong>Summer Solstice (June 21)</strong> in the Northern Hemisphere.</p>\n<p><strong>Bisection of India into Two Climatic Realms:</strong>\nThe Tropic of Cancer passes approximately through the physical center of India, bisecting the country into two roughly equal halves:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Southern Half (Peninsular India):</strong> Lies in the <strong>Tropical Zone</strong> (Torrid Zone). Characterized by high temperatures year-round, low annual range of temperature, and strong maritime moderating influence.</li>\n<li><strong>Northern Half (Extra-Peninsular India):</strong> Lies in the <strong>Subtropical / Warm Temperate Zone</strong>. Characterized by a high annual range of temperature, scorching summers, and distinctly chilly winters with continental weather patterns.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>The Eight Indian States Traversed by the Tropic of Cancer:</strong>\nFrom <strong>West to East</strong>, the Tropic of Cancer passes through exactly <strong>eight Indian states</strong>:\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Gujarat:</strong> Passes through Jasdan, Kutch, Surendranagar, Ahmedabad, Sabarkantha, Aravalli.</li>\n<li><strong>Rajasthan:</strong> Traverses the southernmost tip through <strong>Banswara</strong> and <strong>Dungarpur</strong> districts (shortest length among all states).</li>\n<li><strong>Madhya Pradesh:</strong> Spans the longest distance across 14 districts (Ratlam, Ujjain, Shajapur, Sehore, Bhopal, Vidisha, Raisen, Sagar, Damoh, Jabalpur, Katni, Umaria, Shahdol, Anuppur). The famous historic astronomical observatory at Ujjain (Jantar Mantar) was constructed by Maharaja Sawai Jai Singh precisely because Ujjain was historically viewed as the meridian prime for Hindu astronomical calculations on the Tropic of Cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Chhattisgarh:</strong> Passes through Koriya, Surajpur, and Balrampur districts. (Intersects the $82^\\circ 30'\\text{ E}$ Standard Meridian here).</li>\n<li><strong>Jharkhand:</strong> Traverses Lohardaga, Gumla, Ranchi, Ramgarh, Bokaro.</li>\n<li><strong>West Bengal:</strong> Traverses Purulia, Bankura, Purba Bardhaman, Paschim Bardhaman, Nadia (passes through Krishnanagar).</li>\n<li><strong>Tripura:</strong> Traverses Udaipur (Radhakishorepur).</li>\n<li><strong>Mizoram:</strong> Traverses Champhai.</li>\n</ol>\n</p>\n<p><strong>State Capitals & Cities Near the Tropic of Cancer:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Capital Located Almost Directly on the Line:</strong> <strong>Ranchi</strong> (capital of Jharkhand) is the closest state capital to the Tropic of Cancer ($23^\\circ 21'\\text{ N}$ vs. $23^\\circ 30'\\text{ N}$).</li>\n<li><strong>Capitals Located South of the Line:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Gandhinagar (Gujarat, $23^\\circ 13'\\text{ N}$)</li>\n    <li>Bhopal (Madhya Pradesh, $23^\\circ 15'\\text{ N}$)</li>\n    <li>Kolkata (West Bengal, $22^\\circ 34'\\text{ N}$)</li>\n    <li>Aizawl (Mizoram, $23^\\circ 44'\\text{ N}$ - lies slightly north)</li>\n    <li>Agartala (Tripura, $23^\\circ 50'\\text{ N}$ - lies slightly north)</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><em>City Order (South to North relative proximity):</em> Kolkata ($22^\\circ 34'$) $\\rightarrow$ Gandhinagar ($23^\\circ 13'$) $\\rightarrow$ Bhopal ($23^\\circ 15'$) $\\rightarrow$ Ranchi ($23^\\circ 21'$) $\\rightarrow$ <strong>Tropic of Cancer ($23^\\circ 30'$)</strong> $\\rightarrow$ Aizawl ($23^\\circ 44'$) $\\rightarrow$ Agartala ($23^\\circ 50'$).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>The Unique Phenomenon of the Mahi River:</strong>\nThe <strong>Mahi River</strong> (originating from the Dhar district in the Vindhya Range, Madhya Pradesh) is the <strong>only river in India (and in the world) that cuts across the Tropic of Cancer twice</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>First crossing: As it flows northward from Madhya Pradesh into Rajasthan (Banswara district).</li>\n<li>Second crossing: As it curves southward from Rajasthan into Gujarat before discharging into the Gulf of Khambhat (Arabian Sea).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
