            "id": "m08",
            "title": "India's Borders with Nepal, Bhutan & Myanmar",
            "summary": "India-Nepal Treaty of Peace and Friendship (1950), Treaty of Sugauli (1816), Kalapani dispute on the Mahakali river, India-Bhutan peaceful friendship border, India-Myanmar 1,643 km border along Arakan Yoma, and Free Movement Regime (FMR).",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>1. The India-Nepal Border ($1,751\\text{ km}$):</strong>\nIndia shares an open, porous, and friendly international frontier with the landlocked Himalayan nation of <strong>Nepal</strong> across <strong>five Indian states</strong>: Uttarakhand, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar ($729\\text{ km}$, longest among all states), West Bengal, and Sikkim:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Historical Treaty Foundations:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Treaty of Sugauli (1816):</strong> Signed between the British East India Company and the Kingdom of Nepal following the Anglo-Nepalese War (1814–1816). Article 5 of the Treaty designated the <strong>Kali River (Mahakali / Sarda)</strong> as the western international boundary between British India (Kumaon) and Nepal, and the <strong>Mechi River</strong> as the eastern boundary.</li>\n    <li><strong>Indo-Nepal Treaty of Peace and Friendship (1950):</strong> Established an open border permitting citizens of both sovereign nations free movement without passports or visas, granting national treatment regarding residence, property ownership, and employment.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The Kalapani, Lipulekh & Limpiyadhura Dispute:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>In 2020, Nepal issued a revised political map claiming $\\sim 370\\text{ sq km}$ of territory in Pithoragarh district of Uttarakhand (comprising Kalapani, Limpiyadhura, and Lipulekh Pass).</li>\n    <li><em>Origin of the Conflict:</em> Divergent geographical interpretations regarding the true headwaters and origin of the <strong>Kali River</strong>. Nepal argues that the stream originating at Limpiyadhura is the main Kali River, whereas India asserts that the river originates from springs at Kalapani, which India has garrisoned and administered since the 1950s. Lipulekh Pass is a vital pilgrimage route for the annual <strong>Kailash-Mansarovar Yatra</strong>.</li>\n    <li><em>Susta Dispute:</em> Located in West Champaran district of Bihar; caused by natural shifts in the course of the <strong>Gandak River (Narayani)</strong> over the past two centuries.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>2. The India-Bhutan Border ($699\\text{ km}$):</strong>\nIndia shares a peaceful, stable, and completely demarcated border with the landlocked Buddhist kingdom of <strong>Bhutan</strong> across four Indian states:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>States:</strong> Assam ($267\\text{ km}$, longest), West Bengal ($183\\text{ km}$), Arunachal Pradesh ($217\\text{ km}$), and Sikkim ($32\\text{ km}$).</li>\n<li><strong>Treaty of Friendship (1949, updated 2007):</strong> Mandates perpetual peace, mutual respect for sovereignty, and close strategic economic cooperation (especially in cross-border <strong>Hydroelectric Power Projects</strong> such as Tala, Chukha, Kurichhu, and Mangdechhu). Guarded by the <strong>Sashastra Seema Bal (SSB)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>3. The India-Myanmar Border ($1,643\\text{ km}$):</strong>\nIndia shares its eastern terrestrial land border with <strong>Myanmar (Burma)</strong> across four northeastern Indian states:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>States:</strong> Arunachal Pradesh ($520\\text{ km}$, longest), Nagaland ($215\\text{ km}$), Manipur ($398\\text{ km}$), and Mizoram ($510\\text{ km}$).</li>\n<li><strong>Natural Geographic Frontier:</strong> The boundary is largely natural, running along the mountainous ridgelines of the <strong>Patkai Bum</strong>, <strong>Naga Hills</strong>, <strong>Manipur Hills</strong>, and <strong>Mizo (Lushai) Hills</strong>, which constitute northern extensions of the <strong>Arakan Yoma Range</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The Free Movement Regime (FMR):</strong>\n  Historically instituted to respect shared cross-border ethnic and kinship ties (such as the Kuki-Chin-Mizo and Naga tribes living on both sides). The FMR permitted indigenous border residents to travel up to <strong>$16\\text{ km}$</strong> across the border without a visa and stay for up to two weeks. In early 2024, the Government of India announced the scrapping of the FMR and the fencing of the entire $1,643\\text{ km}$ border to prevent illegal immigration, narcotics trafficking, and cross-border insurgent movement.</li>\n<li>Guarded primarily by the <strong>Assam Rifles</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
