            "title": "Coastal States, Coastline Architecture & Island Territories",
            "summary": "Total coastline length (7,516.6 km), mainland coastline (5,422.6 km), 9 coastal states in descending order (Gujarat, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, Kerala, Odisha, Karnataka, West Bengal, Goa), and 4 coastal Union Territories.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>India's Maritime Coastline:</strong>\nIndia possesses a peninsular coastline that projects deep into the northern Indian Ocean. The total length of the coastline of the Indian Union is exactly <strong>$7,516.6\\text{ km}$</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mainland Coastline:</strong> <strong>$5,422.6\\text{ km}$</strong> ($5,423\\text{ km}$).</li>\n<li><strong>Island Territories Coastline:</strong> <strong>$2,094.0\\text{ km}$</strong> (Andaman & Nicobar Islands: $1,962\\text{ km}$; Lakshadweep: $132\\text{ km}$).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>The Nine Coastal States of India (Descending Order of Coastline Length):</strong>\nIndia's coastline is shared across <strong>nine coastal states</strong>:\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:8px; margin-bottom:12px;\" border=\"1\">\n<tr style=\"background:#f4f4f4;\">\n<th style=\"padding:5px;\">Rank</th>\n<th style=\"padding:5px;\">Coastal State</th>\n<th style=\"padding:5px;\">Coastline Length</th>\n<th style=\"padding:5px;\">Regional Coastal Name</th>\n<th style=\"padding:5px;\">Geographical Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">1</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Gujarat</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$1,214.7\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Kathiawar Coast / Kutch Coast</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Longest mainland coastline</strong> in India. Highly indented with two major gulfs: Gulf of Kutch and Gulf of Khambhat. Maximum tidal range (at Bhavnagar); major salt production center.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">2</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Andhra Pradesh</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$973.7\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Northern Circars (coastal AP)</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Second longest mainland coastline</strong>; longest on the Bay of Bengal / Eastern coast. Deltas of Godavari and Krishna rivers; Visakhapatnam natural harbor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">3</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Tamil Nadu</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$906.9\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Coromandel Coast</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Receives primary rainfall from the Retreating / North-East Monsoon in winter (Oct–Dec); delta of the Kaveri river. Major ports: Chennai, Ennore, Thoothukudi.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">4</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Maharashtra</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$652.6\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Konkan Coast</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Submerged, rocky coast with rias, estuaries, and creeks. Major ports: Mumbai (natural harbor) and Jawaharlal Nehru Port (JNPT, Nhava Sheva).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">5</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Kerala</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$569.7\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Malabar Coast</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Emergent coast characterized by lagoons, backwaters (<strong>Kayals</strong>, e.g., Vembanad Lake, Ashtamudi Lake) connected by canals, and sandspits. Monazite-rich beach sands (Thorium).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">6</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Odisha</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$476.4\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Utkal Coast</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Delta of Mahanadi, Brahmani, and Baitarani rivers; features <strong>Chilika Lake</strong> (Asia's largest brackish water lagoon) and Gahirmatha beach (Olive Ridley turtle nesting). Paradip Port.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">7</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Karnataka</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$280.0\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Canara / Karavali Coast</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Straight coast between Goa and Kerala. Major port: New Mangalore Port.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">8</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>West Bengal</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$157.5\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Sundarban Delta Coast</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Dominated by the world's largest mangrove delta forest; tidal mudflats and distributary estuaries of the Ganga-Brahmaputra. Kolkata (riverine port) and Haldia.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">9</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Goa</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>$101.0\\text{ km}$</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\">Konkan (Goa sector)</td>\n<td style=\"padding:5px;\"><strong>Shortest mainland coastline</strong> among all states. Scenic sandy beaches; estuaries of Mandovi and Zuari rivers; Mormugao Port.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Coastline of the Four Coastal Union Territories:</strong>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Andaman and Nicobar Islands:</strong> <strong>$1,962.0\\text{ km}$</strong> (Longest coastline of any administrative entity in India, exceeding even Gujarat).</li>\n<li><strong>Lakshadweep:</strong> <strong>$132.0\\text{ km}$</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Puducherry:</strong> <strong>$47.6\\text{ km}$</strong> (distributed across 4 enclaves).</li>\n<li><strong>Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu:</strong> <strong>$42.5\\text{ km}$</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</p>\n",
