            "title": "Landlocked States, Enclaves & The Siliguri Corridor",
            "summary": "19 landlocked states and 4 UTs, 5 completely landlocked states with neither international borders nor coastlines (Haryana, MP, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Telangana), and the strategic Siliguri Corridor ('Chicken's Neck').",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Landlocked States & Territories of India:</strong>\nA <strong>landlocked</strong> territory is one that has no direct access or coastline touching the sea or ocean:\n<ul>\n<li>In India, out of 28 States and 8 Union Territories, <strong>19 States and 4 Union Territories</strong> are landlocked.</li>\n<li>Many of these landlocked states touch foreign nations (e.g., Punjab, Rajasthan, Bihar, Uttarakhand, Assam, etc.).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>The Five Completely Landlocked States (Zero International Borders & Zero Coastline):</strong>\nThere are exactly <strong>five Indian states</strong> that are completely landlocked internally within the Indian Union, possessing <strong>neither an international frontier with a foreign country nor a maritime coastline</strong>:\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Haryana:</strong> Landlocked in northern India; surrounded by Punjab, Himachal Pradesh, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh, and NCT of Delhi.</li>\n<li><strong>Madhya Pradesh:</strong> The central heartland state; surrounded by UP, Rajasthan, Gujarat, Maharashtra, and Chhattisgarh.</li>\n<li><strong>Chhattisgarh:</strong> Carved out of MP in 2000; surrounded by UP, Jharkhand, Odisha, Telangana, Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, and MP.</li>\n<li><strong>Jharkhand:</strong> Carved out of Bihar in 2000; surrounded by Bihar, West Bengal, Odisha, Chhattisgarh, and UP.</li>\n<li><strong>Telangana:</strong> Carved out of Andhra Pradesh in 2014; surrounded by Maharashtra, Karnataka, Andhra Pradesh, and Chhattisgarh.</li>\n</ol>\n<em>Union Territories with Zero International Border & Coastline:</em> <strong>NCT of Delhi</strong> and <strong>Chandigarh</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The Geostrategic Siliguri Corridor (\"The Chicken's Neck\"):</strong>\nThe <strong>Siliguri Corridor</strong> is a narrow strip of land situated in the northern part of West Bengal, surrounding the city of Siliguri:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Geographical Dimensions:</strong>\n  It measures approximately <strong>$20\\text{ to }22\\text{ km}$ wide</strong> at its narrowest point (and up to $60\\text{ km}$ at its widest), with a length of roughly $60\\text{ km}$.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategic Role:</strong>\n  It acts as the single terrestrial umbilical cord connecting the main body of India to the <strong>eight Northeastern states</strong> (Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim, and Tripura—often collectively termed the \"North-East\" or the \"Seven Sisters plus Sikkim\").</li>\n<li><strong>Vulnerability & Encirclement:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Bordered on the north by <strong>Nepal</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Bordered on the south by <strong>Bangladesh</strong>.</li>\n    <li>Lies within close proximity ($\\sim 130\\text{ km}$) to China's militarized <strong>Chumbi Valley</strong> (Tibet), wedged between Sikkim and Bhutan.</li>\n    <li>Any hostile military interdiction of this narrow neck would sever terrestrial road, rail, electrical, telecommunication, and petroleum supply lines to the entire Northeast of India.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Infrastructure & Strategic Redundancy:</strong>\n  <ul>\n    <li>Contains the vital broad-gauge railway trunk lines, National Highway 27 (NH-27 / East-West Corridor), high-voltage power transmission lines, and military logistics hubs (33 Corps at Sukna).</li>\n    <li>To mitigate this bottleneck, India is developing alternative multimodal transit routes, such as the <strong>Kaladan Multi-Modal Transit Transport Project</strong> (connecting Kolkata to Sittwe port in Myanmar, then via river and highway to Mizoram) and the <strong>Chittagong and Mongla Port access agreements</strong> with Bangladesh.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
