            "summary": "State area rankings (Rajasthan largest, Goa smallest), UT area rankings (Ladakh largest, Lakshadweep smallest), population extremes (UP vs Sikkim), district area extremes (Kutch largest, Mahe smallest), and demographic density extremes.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Geographical Superlatives of Indian States & Territories:</strong>\nFor civil services examinations, exact spatial superlatives regarding area, administrative scale, and demographics are essential core knowledge:</p>\n<p>\n<table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:8px; margin-bottom:12px;\" border=\"1\">\n<tr style=\"background:#f4f4f4;\">\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Geographical Parameter</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Largest / Highest Entity</th>\n<th style=\"padding:6px;\">Smallest / Lowest Entity</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>State by Geographical Area</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Rajasthan</strong> ($342,239\\text{ sq km}$, $\\sim 10.41\\%$ of India)<br><em>Top 4 Order:</em> 1. Rajasthan > 2. Madhya Pradesh ($308,245\\text{ sq km}$) > 3. Maharashtra ($307,713\\text{ sq km}$) > 4. Uttar Pradesh ($240,928\\text{ sq km}$).</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Goa</strong> ($3,702\\text{ sq km}$, $0.11\\%$ of India)<br><em>Ascending Order:</em> 1. Goa < 2. Sikkim ($7,096\\text{ sq km}$) < 3. Tripura ($10,491\\text{ sq km}$).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Union Territory by Area</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Ladakh</strong> ($\\sim 59,146\\text{ sq km}$ Indian administered; total claims $166,698\\text{ sq km}$)<br>Followed by Jammu & Kashmir and Andaman & Nicobar.</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Lakshadweep</strong> ($32\\text{ sq km}$ total land area across 36 atolls).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>District by Geographical Area</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Kutch</strong> (Gujarat, $45,674\\text{ sq km}$)<br>Followed by <strong>Leh</strong> (Ladakh, $45,110\\text{ sq km}$) and Jaisalmer (Rajasthan, $38,401\\text{ sq km}$).</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Mahe</strong> (Puducherry, $9\\text{ sq km}$).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>State by Human Population (2011 Census)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Uttar Pradesh</strong> ($199.8\\text{ million}$, $\\sim 16.51\\%$ of India)<br>Followed by Maharashtra ($112.4\\text{ M}$) and Bihar ($104.1\\text{ M}$).</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Sikkim</strong> ($610,577$ citizens, least populous state)<br>Followed by Mizoram ($1.09\\text{ M}$) and Arunachal Pradesh ($1.38\\text{ M}$).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Union Territory by Population</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>NCT of Delhi</strong> ($16.78\\text{ million}$, 2011 Census).</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Lakshadweep</strong> ($64,473$ citizens).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Population Density (Persons / sq km)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Bihar</strong> ($1,106\\text{ persons/sq km}$, highest among states)<br><em>Among UTs:</em> <strong>Delhi</strong> ($11,320\\text{ persons/sq km}$).</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Arunachal Pradesh</strong> ($17\\text{ persons/sq km}$, lowest among states)<br><em>Among UTs:</em> <strong>Andaman & Nicobar</strong> ($46\\text{ persons/sq km}$).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Sex Ratio (Females per 1,000 Males)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Kerala</strong> ($1,084$ females per 1,000 males).</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Haryana</strong> ($879$ females per 1,000 males, lowest among states).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Literacy Rate (%)</strong></td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Kerala</strong> ($94.00\\%$, highest in India).</td>\n<td style=\"padding:6px;\"><strong>Bihar</strong> ($61.80\\%$, 2011 Census).</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</p>\n<p><strong>States Bordering the Most Other States:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Uttar Pradesh:</strong> Touches the maximum number of neighboring administrative units—exactly <strong>8 States and 1 Union Territory</strong> (9 entities in total):\n  <ol>\n    <li>Uttarakhand (northwest)</li>\n    <li>Himachal Pradesh (northernmost tip in Saharanpur)</li>\n    <li>Haryana (west)</li>\n    <li>NCT of Delhi (west)</li>\n    <li>Rajasthan (southwest)</li>\n    <li>Madhya Pradesh (south)</li>\n    <li>Chhattisgarh (south)</li>\n    <li>Jharkhand (southeast)</li>\n    <li>Bihar (east)</li>\n  </ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Assam:</strong> Touches <strong>7 states</strong> (West Bengal, Arunachal Pradesh, Nagaland, Manipur, Mizoram, Tripura, Meghalaya).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n",
